# Manual voice and pacing — NON-NEGOTIABLE

Audience: someone new. Write as if explaining to a classmate, not an engine programmer.

## Punctuation

- **Never use the em dash (—).** Not in prose, tables, or list suffixes.
- **Never use Unicode arrows (→ ← ⇒ etc.).** Use ASCII **`->`**, **`<-`**, or **`=>`** (example: **File -> Settings**, **Music -> Master Volume**). See **`.cursor/rules/ascii-markdown.mdc`**.
- Use a **colon** when the second part explains the first (example: **`on_ready`**: runs after init).
- Use a **hyphen** with spaces when you need a break (example: HUD **-** crosshair and prompts).
- Normal **`-`** in markdown bullets and in API names is fine. Only the long em dash character is banned.

## Plain language

- Short sentences. One main idea per paragraph.
- Prefer **“you”** and concrete outcomes (“resize the window”, “click a button”) over abstract architecture essays.
- Use **tables** for “when to use which” (HUD vs menu, scale modes, layers).
- **`reference/`** may stay dry and catalog-like. **`manuals/`**, **`tutorials/`**, and **`getting_started/`** stay approachable.

## Terminology

- **Use Detis / engine names.** Verify against stubs, the editor UI, and **`reference/`**. Do not invent labels or borrow generic gamedev words when the product already has a term.
- **World, not scene.** A loaded **`.world`** and everything in it is a **world**. Say **world script**, **world load**, **current world**. Do not call it a scene, level, or map unless you are quoting user speech and immediately map it to **world**.
- Use names readers will see in Lua and stubs: **`gui_begin_design_canvas`**, **reference resolution**, **design space**, **foreground draw**, **normalized**, **`world_*`**, **`entity_*`**.
- Do not lean on third-party UI library jargon in user-facing pages when an engine term exists.
- Bold **`api_like_this`** when naming functions or enums the reader will type.

## Teach the simple path first

- Show the **minimum** that works, then optional polish.
- Example: **`gui_begin_design_canvas(name, 0)`** before long **`GuiWindowFlags`** lists. Mention flag bundles only when discussing look-and-feel, with a pointer to a sample file (example: **`gui_demo_skin.lua`**) rather than front-loading every flag in the first example.
- Split **init once** (**`on_ready`**: reference resolution) from **each frame** (**`on_draw`**: draw calls) when both are required.

## Sample vs product

- State clearly once per page (or in a dedicated section): shipped **`content/scripts/`** and worlds like **`gui_demo.world`** are **optional reference**, not requirements.
- In code examples, teach **`gui_*`** (the engine). Helpers like **`UiWidgets`** are “if you copy sample code”, not the default path in every fence.
- Do not repeat “demo” on every line; let the code and API names speak.

## Depth and linking

- Manuals teach **how to think and what to call first**. Exhaustive function lists live in **`reference/`** or engine repo **`docs/game/`**.
- Link out instead of duplicating huge API tables on beginner pages.
- Multi-page guides use one **`## Continue reading`** footer with **Previous** and **Next** lines (see **`.cursor/rules/sequential-navigation.mdc`**). No **Related** link dumps.

## Release labels and notes

- Do not label docs with release phase or version (alpha, beta, RC, build numbers).
- Use **`> **Note:**`** for brief limitation callouts. Do not use **Early Alpha Notice** or similar version-tied banners.

## Respect existing pages

- Do not rewrite user-edited wording or section order unless the task asks for it.
- Extend examples and fix accuracy; avoid drive-by renames or “cleanup” refactors.
